B.P8=new A.aQ("What percentage of the gait cycle is stance phase vs swing phase?","Stance phase = 60% of the gait cycle. Swing phase = 40%. Double limb support = 20% (10% at the beginning and 10% at the end of stance).")
B.Ol=new A.aQ("What are the 8 subphases of the gait cycle?","Stance (5): 1) Initial contact, 2) Loading response, 3) Midstance, 4) Terminal stance, 5) Pre-swing. Swing (3): 6) Initial swing, 7) Mid-swing, 8) Terminal swing.")
B.Oc=new A.aQ("What is the indication for a Posterior Leaf Spring (PLS) AFO?","Foot drop (dorsiflexor weakness) with intact quadriceps and no significant mediolateral instability. It provides dorsiflexion assist during swing phase. Lightweight, fits in most shoes.")
B.OE=new A.aQ("What is the indication for a Floor Reaction Orthosis (FRO)?","Knee extensor weakness with intact quadriceps. The rigid AFO set in slight plantarflexion creates a ground reaction force anterior to the knee, producing a knee extension moment during stance. REQUIRES intact quads to function.")
B.O1=new A.aQ("What is the indication for a Solid Ankle AFO?","Mediolateral ankle instability, spasticity, or combined sagittal and frontal plane instability. Blocks all ankle motion in all planes. Provides maximum control but most restrictive.")
B.NZ=new A.aQ("Which cervical orthosis best restricts flexion? What percentage?","SOMI (Sternal Occipital Mandibular Immobilizer) restricts cervical flexion approximately 93%. However, it only restricts extension about 65%. The halo vest is the gold standard for OVERALL motion restriction.")
B.NY=new A.aQ("What spinal levels does a TLSO cover?","T6 to L3. Cannot effectively control above T6 because the rib cage limits corrective force application. For higher thoracic control, a CTLSO with cervical extension is needed.")
B.P4=new A.aQ("Williams flexion orthosis vs Jewett hyperextension orthosis: indications?","Williams = promotes lumbar FLEXION, restricts extension. For: spondylolisthesis, spinal stenosis, facet arthropathy. Jewett = promotes thoracolumbar EXTENSION via 3-point system. For: stable anterior compression fractures (T6-L3). Contraindicated for posterior element fractures.")
B.O3=new A.aQ("Nerve injury splint matching: radial, ulnar, median?","Radial nerve = Wrist drop splint (wrist extension orthosis). Ulnar nerve = Anti-claw splint (MCP flexion block). Median nerve = Opponens splint (thumb abduction orthosis). Mnemonic: RUMble - Radial/Ulnar/Median.")
B.P_=new A.aQ("What is the safe (intrinsic plus) position for a resting hand splint?","Wrist: 20-30 degrees extension. MCPs: 60-70 degrees flexion. IPs: slight flexion. Thumb: palmar abduction. This position keeps collateral ligaments at maximum length to prevent contracture.")
B.OF=new A.aQ("What is a Thomas heel shoe modification?","The medial border of the heel is extended anteriorly by 1/2 to 3/4 inch. Provides medial longitudinal arch support. Used for pes planus (flatfoot) and pronation problems.")
B.OL=new A.aQ("What is a rocker bottom sole and when is it indicated?","A convex sole that facilitates forward progression. Indicated for limited ankle or MTP joint motion (hallux rigidus, ankle arthrodesis). Compensates for loss of normal forefoot motion during push-off.")
B.OP=new A.aQ("What does the UCBL orthosis treat?","University of California Biomechanics Laboratory orthosis: a rigid foot orthosis that cups the heel and extends along medial/lateral borders. For FLEXIBLE pes planus only. Maintains subtalar neutral and supports medial longitudinal arch. NOT effective for rigid flatfoot.")
B.O6=new A.aQ("KAFO vs AFO: what muscle weakness determines the need?","KAFO needed when quadriceps (knee extensors) are weak and the knee buckles during stance. AFO sufficient when quadriceps are intact and only ankle/foot control is needed. The knee is the deciding joint.")
B.OW=new A.aQ("Milwaukee brace vs Boston brace for scoliosis?","Milwaukee brace (CTLSO): extends to occiput, for high thoracic curves with apex at or above T7. Boston brace (underarm TLSO): for curves with apex below T7. Boston is more commonly used due to better cosmesis and patient compliance.")
B.NT=new A.aQ("What is a SOMI orthosis and what does it best control?","Sternal Occipital Mandibular Immobilizer. Uses sternal plate, occipital piece, and mandibular support. Best for cervical FLEXION restriction (93%). Used for upper-to-mid cervical fractures where flexion control is the primary concern.")
B.OO=new A.aQ("Swan neck vs boutonniere deformity: describe each and the appropriate splint.","Swan neck: PIP hyperextension + DIP flexion. Splint: silver ring blocking PIP hyperextension. Boutonniere: PIP flexion + DIP hyperextension (from central slip disruption). Splint: PIP extension splint.")
B.OT=new A.aQ("What orthotic level is needed for an L4 spinal cord injury?","Bilateral AFOs. L4 preserves quadriceps (L2-L4) and hip flexion but loses dorsiflexors and plantarflexors. Higher injuries: L2-L3 may need KAFOs (insufficient quads). Thoracic levels need HKAFOs or RGOs.")
B.OX=new A.aQ("What is the Kleinert protocol for flexor tendon repair?","Dorsal blocking splint with rubber band traction. Holds wrist and MCPs in flexion to protect the repair. Rubber bands provide passive flexion; active extension is allowed to a limited range. Promotes tendon healing while preventing adhesions.")
B.OG=new A.aQ("What are the characteristics of a stance-control KAFO vs a locked-knee KAFO?","Stance-control KAFO: automatically locks during stance and unlocks during swing, allowing free knee flexion for a more normal gait. Locked-knee KAFO: knee stays rigid throughout gait cycle, requiring circumduction or hip-hike to clear the limb. Stance-control reduces energy expenditure.")
B.ON=new A.aQ("What is the Jewett orthosis three-point pressure system?","Anterior sternal pad + anterior suprapubic pad + posterior thoracolumbar pad. Creates an extension moment. Used for stable anterior compression fractures T6-L3. Contraindicated for posterior element fractures (extension would stress posterior structures).")
