B.Os=new A.aQ("What are the Medicare K-levels (K0-K4)?","K0: Cannot ambulate or transfer safely. K1: Household ambulator (limited/fixed cadence). K2: Limited community ambulator (low-level activity). K3: Community ambulator (variable cadence, can traverse barriers). K4: Exceeds basic ambulation (high impact, sports, children).")
B.O5=new A.aQ("Match prosthetic foot type to K-level.","SACH foot = K1 (household). Single-axis = K1-K2. Multi-axis = K2-K3. Dynamic response / energy-storing = K3-K4. Microprocessor = K3-K4. Running-specific = K4.")
B.P1=new A.aQ("VO (voluntary opening) vs VC (voluntary closing) terminal devices?","VO: rubber bands hold device closed, user pulls cable to open. Provides constant holding force without effort. More common. VC: user pulls cable to close/grip, providing proportional grip strength. More natural prehension but requires sustained effort to maintain grip.")
B.On=new A.aQ("PTB socket: pressure-tolerant vs pressure-sensitive areas?","TOLERANT: patellar tendon (primary), medial tibial flare, anterolateral muscular compartment, popliteal fossa. SENSITIVE (need relief): tibial crest, fibular head (peroneal nerve!), distal tibia, hamstring tendons, cut end of fibula.")
B.NV=new A.aQ("Quadrilateral vs IRC (ischial containment) socket?","Quad: ischium sits ON TOP of flat posterior brim. Wider ML than AP. IRC: ischium contained WITHIN the socket walls. Narrower ML, wider AP. IRC provides better rotational control and more anatomical femoral alignment.")
B.Oi=new A.aQ("Manual locking knee: when is it used?","Last resort option. Used for patients with very poor balance, strength, or cognition who cannot safely control a free-swinging knee. Eliminates knee flexion during walking (stiff-legged gait). Patient must circumduct or hip-hike. Can unlock manually for sitting.")
B.OQ=new A.aQ("Why is a polycentric knee ideal for knee disarticulation?","The polycentric (four-bar) linkage shortens the shank during swing phase, improving cosmesis when seated (avoids the long thigh segment protruding). Also provides inherent stance stability because the instantaneous center of rotation moves posteriorly during flexion.")
B.Oo=new A.aQ("Ideal transtibial amputation level and fibula length?","Ideal TT level: junction of proximal and middle thirds (proximal 50%) of the tibia. Fibula cut 2-3 cm shorter than the tibia. Provides adequate lever arm while ensuring sufficient soft tissue coverage for socket fitting.")
B.OM=new A.aQ("Body-powered vs myoelectric prosthesis: advantages of each?","Body-powered: lighter, cheaper, more durable, waterproof, proprioceptive feedback through cable, easier maintenance. Myoelectric: greater grip strength (20-30 lbs vs 5-7 lbs), better cosmesis, no harness needed, less compensatory body motion.")
B.Ox=new A.aQ("What does suction suspension require for optimal function?","Stable residual limb volume. Creates negative pressure seal between socket and limb (via sealing liner or elevated vacuum). Volume fluctuation breaks the seal, causing pistoning. Provides excellent proprioception when properly fitted on a mature limb.")
B.OV=new A.aQ("What is a Syme amputation?","Ankle disarticulation with preserved heel pad. Heel pad secured to distal tibia for end-bearing. Characteristic bulbous distal end (malleoli). Provides longer lever arm than standard TT. Excellent weight-bearing tolerance. Prosthetic cosmesis can be challenging.")
B.NW=new A.aQ("What is myodesis vs myoplasty?","Myodesis: muscle sutured directly to BONE through drill holes. Provides secure fixation, maintains muscle tension and function. Better prosthetic control. Myoplasty: agonist and antagonist muscles sutured TO EACH OTHER over the bone end. Simpler but less secure fixation.")
B.OD=new A.aQ("TKA line alignment for transfemoral prosthetic knee?","Trochanter-Knee-Ankle line. Prosthetic knee axis placed slightly ANTERIOR to TKA line for stance stability. More anterior = more stable but harder swing initiation. More posterior = easier swing but less stable. Finding balance is key to alignment.")
B.Ou=new A.aQ("Pin lock suspension: best for whom? Limitations?","Best for: active patients and athletes. Provides secure, reliable suspension during high activity. Pin on distal gel liner engages shuttle lock in socket. Limitation: not ideal for significant volume changes (fixed distal attachment). Easy to don with audible click.")
B.OS=new A.aQ("What is the TSB (Total Surface Bearing) socket?","Distributes weight uniformly across the entire residual limb surface (vs PTB which concentrates load on specific areas). Uses gel liners for hydrostatic loading. Reduces peak pressures. More comfortable for many patients. Modern standard for transtibial sockets.")
B.Or=new A.aQ("What is elevated vacuum suspension?","Uses electric or mechanical pump to maintain sub-atmospheric pressure between liner and socket. Benefits: reduces pistoning to near zero, maintains limb volume throughout day, improves proprioception and gait symmetry. Requires stable limb, compatible components.")
B.OZ=new A.aQ("Two-site myoelectric control for transradial prosthesis?","Surface electrodes placed over wrist FLEXORS and EXTENSORS. Flexor contraction = hand close. Extensor contraction = hand open. EMG signal amplitude determines movement speed. For higher-level amputations, different muscle pairs are used.")
B.P7=new A.aQ("Body-powered prosthesis: what motion activates the control cable?","Glenohumeral flexion and scapular abduction (protraction). These motions pull the cable via a figure-of-eight or figure-of-nine harness. For higher-level amputations, additional cables and motions control elbow lock and forearm lift independently.")
B.O0=new A.aQ("Microprocessor knee: primary advantage and indication?","Advantage: real-time adjustment of swing and stance resistance based on speed, terrain, and activity via sensors and onboard processor. More natural gait, improved stability on uneven surfaces, reduced falls. Indication: K3-K4 ambulators. Requires battery charging.")
B.OC=new A.aQ("What is a Chopart amputation vs Lisfranc amputation?","Chopart: disarticulation through midtarsal joint (talonavicular and calcaneocuboid). Preserves talus and calcaneus. Tends toward equinovarus deformity. Lisfranc: disarticulation through tarsometatarsal joints. Preserves entire hindfoot and midfoot.")
B.Wv=s([B.Os,B.O5,B.P1,B.On,B.NV,B.Oi,B.OQ,B.Oo,B.OM,B.Ox,B.OV,B.NW,B.OD,B.Ou,B.OS,B.Or,B.OZ,B.P7,B.O0,B.OC],t.x7)
B.OR=new A.aQ("Lateral trunk bending toward prosthetic side during stance: causes?","Most common: weak hip abductors (gluteus medius) or prosthesis too short. Patient shifts trunk over prosthetic side to move center of gravity over base of support. Other causes: laterally placed socket, discomfort, wide ML socket. Trendelenburg-like pattern.")
B.P6=new A.aQ("Circumduction during prosthetic swing: causes?","Most common: prosthesis too long or insufficient knee flexion during swing. Patient swings limb outward in an arc to clear the ground. Other causes: locked knee, excessive knee stability, weak hip flexors, suspension failure causing limb to drop.")
B.OI=new A.aQ("What is vaulting and what causes it?","Rising on the toe of the SOUND foot during prosthetic swing to gain clearance. Causes: prosthesis too long, inadequate suspension (prosthesis drops), insufficient knee flexion. Increases energy expenditure. Address through proper alignment and suspension.")
B.O8=new A.aQ("What causes a medial vs lateral whip in a transfemoral prosthesis?","Both caused by rotational malalignment between socket, knee, and foot. Medial whip (heel kicks IN): knee externally rotated relative to line of progression. Lateral whip (heel kicks OUT): knee internally rotated. Correct by adjusting rotational alignment.")
B.Ow=new A.aQ("Excessive knee flexion (drop-off) during TT prosthetic stance: causes?","Socket set in too much flexion or heel cushion (SACH) too soft. Both direct the ground reaction force posterior to the knee, creating a flexion moment. Patient feels unstable, reports knee buckling. Fix: reduce socket flexion or use firmer heel.")
B.Ot=new A.aQ("What is terminal impact in prosthetic gait?","Abrupt, audible extension of the prosthetic knee at the end of swing phase (just before initial contact). Caused by insufficient friction or extension resistance in the knee unit. Knee swings too freely. Fix: increase extension dampening in the knee.")
B.OJ=new A.aQ("Unequal step length: longer step on prosthetic side. Why?","Patient spends less time on the prosthesis during stance (limited confidence, pain, instability). Takes a shorter step with the sound limb to quickly return to the more secure intact leg. Indicates the patient needs more prosthetic training or adjustment.")
B.Ov=new A.aQ("Wide-based gait in transfemoral prosthesis: causes?","Excessive abduction alignment of prosthesis, excessively wide socket, or long residual limb creating lateral distal bulge. Increases lateral displacement of center of gravity and wastes energy.")
B.Od=new A.aQ("Normal adult cadence and step length?","Cadence: 110-120 steps/minute (about 60 strides/min). Women slightly higher than men. Step length: approximately 72 cm (28 inches). Stride length (two steps): approximately 144 cm. Comfortable walking speed: approximately 1.3 m/s.")
B.Oe=new A.aQ("What muscles are active during loading response?","Quadriceps (eccentric to control knee flexion for shock absorption). Tibialis anterior (eccentric to control plantarflexion). Hip extensors (to stabilize the hip). Loading response = 0-10% of gait cycle = first double limb support period.")
B.O_=new A.aQ("What is the determinant of gait: pelvic rotation?","Pelvis rotates approximately 4 degrees anteriorly on the swing side. This effectively lengthens the limb, smoothing the trajectory of the center of gravity and reducing vertical displacement. One of the six determinants of gait described by Saunders et al.")
B.O2=new A.aQ("Foot flat to midstance: what is the ankle doing?",'Ankle is dorsiflexing (tibial advancement over the fixed foot). Controlled by eccentric contraction of the plantarflexors (soleus primarily). This is the "second rocker" of the three rockers of normal gait. Ground reaction force moves from posterior to anterior.')
B.Om=new A.aQ("What prosthetic gait deviation results from weak hip flexors?","Decreased step length on the prosthetic side. Hip flexors are critical for initiating swing phase. Patient may compensate with increased trunk flexion, lateral lean, or use momentum. May also see reduced walking speed and increased energy expenditure.")
B.O4=new A.aQ("What is the effect of excessive prosthetic foot dorsiflexion on gait?","Causes the knee to flex excessively during midstance (ground reaction force passes posterior to knee). Patient feels unstable, like the knee is buckling forward. May also cause increased pressure on the anterior distal socket. Fix: realign foot toward plantarflexion.")
B.Oz=new A.aQ("Pistoning in a prosthesis: what is it and what causes it?","Vertical movement of the residual limb within the socket. Causes: inadequate suspension, loose socket fit, volume changes. Results in reduced proprioception, skin irritation, and decreased control. Measured in mm of vertical displacement during swing.")
B.O7=new A.aQ("What causes knee hyperextension (recurvatum) in a TT prosthesis during stance?","Socket set in too much extension (insufficient flexion), heel cushion too firm, or foot aligned too far anterior. Ground reaction force passes too far anterior to the knee, creating excessive extension moment. Can cause knee pain and joint damage over time.")
B.Oy=new A.aQ("Rotation of the prosthetic foot during swing: causes?","Poor socket fit (rotational instability), inadequate suspension, or excessive soft tissue allowing the limb to rotate within the socket. The foot appears to rotate inward or outward during swing. Fix: improve socket fit, check suspension, consider a more intimate socket design.")
B.OH=new A.aQ("What are the three rockers of normal gait?","First rocker (heel rocker): heel contact to foot flat, ankle plantarflexes. Second rocker (ankle rocker): foot flat to heel off, tibia advances over foot, ankle dorsiflexes. Third rocker (forefoot rocker): heel off to toe off, MTP extension, push-off.")
B.OK=new A.aQ("Prosthetic gait: what does excessive knee extension resistance cause?","Terminal impact (heel hits ground hard due to rapid extension), difficulty initiating swing, and the patient may compensate with hip hiking or circumduction. The knee unit does not flex smoothly at toe-off, making swing phase initiation labored.")
B.NX=new A.aQ("What does inadequate initial socket flexion cause in a TT prosthesis?","The socket should be set in 5-10 degrees of flexion to approximate the natural tibial position. Insufficient flexion causes knee hyperextension during stance, excessive pressure on the patellar tendon, and loss of the normal knee flexion/extension pattern during gait.")
